      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapters we studied the different ways humanity developed counting methods—whether through addition or multiplication—and still cannot claim that the subject is exhausted. In daily life we often add and multiply without even noticing.\n\nThis text shows that reading and writing are already mathematical activities. We will count the symbols of a poem, measure the syllables of its verses, and read the hieroglyphs of a Maya stela.\n\nWith this in mind, reflect before proceeding:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: On the Texts</b><br /><br />How do you believe addition and multiplication relate to the texts you read?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_overview",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### An Overview\n\nWhenever we read or write something, we are performing mathematical operations that, being so natural, go unnoticed. Let us explore this in more detail.\n\n#### The Sum in Text\n\nOften, because we are so focused on understanding what we're reading, we don't notice that we're summing letters to reach a final result: the comprehension of a word or text (<htcite0>). One possible reason we don't observe this summation could be that we don't use the addition symbol (+) during reading.\n\n#### Multiplication\n\nWhen we combine letter by letter to write or read a word, we're also applying the multiplication table of 1 (<htcite1>). As with summation, we don't insert the multiplication symbol (×) in this interpretation, though it is implicit.\n\nMultiplication also helps us navigate through text. As mentioned in the first text of the chapter on multiplication, to multiply is to perform repeated addition of a quantity (<htcite2>). Since texts are organized in lines — sometimes called verses (<htcite3>) — moving from one line to the next can be interpreted as performing a multiplication. An example of text in verses is Gustavo Dourado's poem *LABUTA*, presented below (<htcite4>; <htcite5>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "e3bb8c74-d982-44a6-8360-f98867c6b7c7",
                     "text": "The Brittanica Dictionary",
