            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text we have seen that mathematics appears in texts explicitly (when we count syllables) and implicitly (when we combine letters to form words, or move from one line to the next). Applying these concepts to texts leads us to a final reflection:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: On Numbers</b><br /><br />When we write numbers, do we also use mathematical operations?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In the next text we will answer this reflection in detail.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Is the following statement correct: <i>\"Texts never mix with math\"</i>?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "Mathematics is present in every area of life, usually in implicit ways: reading a word, moving from one line to the next, or counting the syllables of a verse are all mathematical operations."
      },
      {
         "question": "Can we count metrical syllables with multiplication?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "Counting the metrical syllables of a verse uses the multiplication table of 1. If every verse of the poem keeps the same meter, we can multiply instead of adding: 14 verses × 5 syllables = 70 syllables in total."
      },
      {
         "question": "Can we apply multiplication to hieroglyphs?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "The stela shows 2 columns of hieroglyphs across 9 lines, so 2 × 9 = 18 hieroglyphs. Whenever the same quantity repeats a fixed number of times, multiplication gives the total directly."
      }
   ],
